'''arthroderm''' (är′thrọ̄-dėrm), ''n.'' [< Gr. ἄρθρον, a joint, + δέρμα, skin.] The crust or body-wall of an articulate animal, as the shell of a crab or the integument of an insect. ''A. S. Packard.''

'''arthrodia''' (är-thrō′di-ä), ''n.''; pl. ''arthrodiæ'' (-ē). [NL., < Gr. ἀρθρωδία, a particular kind of articulation, < ἀρθρώδης, articulated, < ἄρθρον, a joint, + εἶδος, form.] A gliding joint; a movable articulation formed by plane or nearly plane surfaces which slide upon each other to some extent, as in the articulations of the carpus: a form of diarthrosis. Also called ''adarticulation.''—'''Double arthrodia.''' {{smaller|Same as ''amphidiarthrosis.''}}

'''arthrodial''' (är-thrō′di-al), ''a.'' [< ''arthrodia'' + ''-al.''] '''1.''' Pertaining to or characterized by an arthrodia.—'''2.''' Of or pertaining to arthrosis; concerned in the jointing or articulation of parts, especially of limbs.—'''Arthrodial apophysis,''' {{smaller|in ''Crustacea,'' that process of an endosternite or endopleurite which enters into the formation of an articular cavity of a limb.}}
{{smaller block|The endopleurite . . . divides into three apophyses, one descending or ''arthrodial,'' and two which pass nearly horizontally inward. {{right|''Huxley,'' Anat. Invert., p. 269.}}}}

'''arthrodic''' (är-throd′ik), ''a.'' Same as ''arthrodial.''

'''arthrodynia''' (är-thrọ̄-din′i-ä), ''n.'' [NL., < Gr. ἄρθρον, a joint, + ὀδύνη, pain.] Pain in a joint; arthralgia.

